WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.650
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to be speaking
with Chris Tarbell, former FBI special agent

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:12.250
and now founding partner at Nexo, was involved
in the eventual takedown of the Silk Road,

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.389
which I've spoken a little bit about before.

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:16.119
Chris, great to have you on today.

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:17.119
Really appreciate it.

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:18.180
Speaker 2: Oh, thanks for having me on, David.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.180
I appreciate it.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:25.840
Speaker 1: So, you know, to start just somewhere
as I read about the story of how Ross Ulbricht

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.340
and the Silk Road were ultimately taken down,
one of the things that is often described

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.489
as the story is told in many of the books
about it is that it's really like the prototypical

00:00:35.489 --> 00:00:41.301
example of different law enforcement agencies
in the U.S. not working well together, which

00:00:41.301 --> 00:00:45.899
is sort of like this narrative that often
exists about, well, the FBI wants credit.

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:50.110
Well, this also involved HCI and involved
all these different agencies.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:56.000
And the Silk Road would have been taken down
much sooner were egos put aside.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.680
That's a story that's often told.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.010
Do you think that that's fair as far as the
way that it went?

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:02.899
Speaker 2: No, not exactly.

00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:06.150
You know, the other agencies were doing good
investigations.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:11.200
HCI had good investigations going as far as
going up the chain.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.370
But unfortunately, this is a new way of dealing
drugs.

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:18.980
I mean, this was you know, they were the buyers
didn't know who the dealers were.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:24.100
The dealers didn't know the admins were, you
know, And so it really was just, you know,

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.600
it didn't go the normal investigative route.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.119
You know, when did this new cyber route?

00:01:28.119 --> 00:01:33.050
Speaker 1: So for people who don't know what
the Silk Road was, can you give them sort

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:34.050
of a brief synopsis?

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:40.280
I mean, it was it was a dark Web site that
was originally, I believe, dealing mostly

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.740
in illegal drugs, but actually came to be
much more than that.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:44.870
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:51.320
It was an online dark market Web site operated
on the Tor network, the Onion Router.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.600
It was a hidden network developed by the Navy
back in the late nineties.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.710
And you can't trace ships traditionally through
it.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:02.310
It's it works on a different protocol that
kind of hides where the traffic is coming

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:03.370
from and going to.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:07.570
But it also worked through anonymity, through
cryptocurrency.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:12.290
This was the early stages of Bitcoin, so you
literally could go on there and buy almost

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:13.290
anything.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:15.750
It was a $1.2 billion empire by the time we
took it down.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.120
Now, there were two things you couldn't buy
on Silk Road.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.489
You couldn't buy guns because that was on
a different website and you also couldn't

00:02:22.489 --> 00:02:26.879
buy fake IDs or sorry, fake college degrees.

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:27.989
You could buy fake IDs.

00:02:27.989 --> 00:02:30.040
You couldn't get a fake college degree.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:35.880
They didn't want you to be like a doctor and
and not really have the education.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:44.430
Speaker 1: When this started to become an
investigation that involved multiple agencies.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.629
What was really or let me put it a different
way.

00:02:47.629 --> 00:02:51.970
What were their concerns about where it could
go?

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:56.360
Or was the concern really what was already
taking place with it?

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:01.760
Speaker 2: Really it was what was already
taking place, you know, in the FBI.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.770
And I can't speak for any desire or Iris or
DEA in their investigations.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:11.890
The FBI investigation really came out of we
had a lot of cases around that time.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.670
It was 2011, 2012.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.170
A lot of cases just ended.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.260
It was tore.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.260
We can't do anything about it.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:25.730
So we had just done a big case involving Anonymous
and the arresting of Sabu, Hector Montecore

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:30.120
and one of the other guys on on the anonymous
case was a guy named Jeremy Hayman, and he

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.240
was arrested and we were using Tor.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.330
So it was one of those interesting things
we didn't do anything with.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.330
Tor just happened.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:38.330
That's how he's hiding.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:39.770
And it took us an extra six months to find
him.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:43.180
But once that case was over, we started sitting
around and thinking about maybe we should

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:44.600
take a look at Tor and see if we can do.

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:49.629
There's so many cases being closed just because
it comes up as a Tor IP address.

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:56.040
Speaker 1: If I recall correctly, a big kind
of breaking point in starting to look at Ross

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:57.150
Ulbricht.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:01.670
Was someone involved in the investigation
and it's been a little while since since I

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:09.340
read the book about the investigation, someone
put together an old handle that Ross used

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:15.920
on some message board, which then they figured
out, Oh, that's the I recognize that that's

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.209
also appearing elsewhere.

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:19.680
Remind us of that detail.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.530
But maybe more importantly, how long did the
Silk Road investigation go before the name

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.890
Ross Ulbricht ever actually was mentioned?

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:29.810
Speaker 2: Well, So there's a there's a path
there.

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:32.850
There's a story there that, you know, it's
kind of difficult.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:39.500
So there we the connecting the word, the name
you're looking for is frosty.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:40.500
Yes.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:46.280
So someone on a on a message board way back
at the start of Silk Road posted, Hey, I'm

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:51.870
starting this onion site and it's going to
use cryptocurrency if anybody has any programing

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:57.070
to help reach out to me at Frosty and I was
posted in the username Frosty in the forums,

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:58.070
right?

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:00.870
Is that reach out to me at Ross dot Ulbricht
at gmail.com.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:01.870
Right.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:03.729
Which again is a clue in our world.

00:05:03.729 --> 00:05:08.470
Yes Not until we found the server and was
able to go through sort of the networks of

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:14.340
the server and find that there was a backup
server in Philadelphia.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:18.621
And then there was a computer that was reaching
out to the backup server in the name of the

00:05:18.621 --> 00:05:21.669
computer was frosty.

00:05:21.669 --> 00:05:26.550
So now we have, you know, this this computer
being run by DPR, Dread Pirate Roberts, the

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:32.770
guy who operated Silk Road, you know, and
then his posting of From Frosty with the email

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:35.150
address, Ross Ulbricht at gmail.com.

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:40.340
Speaker 1: And that was originally the way
that the investigation turned to look specifically

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:41.340
at Ulbricht.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:44.960
Speaker 2: Yeah, I mean, we had to develop
a few other things before we knew that he

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:50.900
was in the Pacific Coast time because there
was a Jabber server that he had set up.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:54.970
That's a communication server for his admins
to talk on, and we knew that his timeframe

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:59.550
was around then, but that specifically, that
was where we connected Ross through that frosty

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:00.550
connection.

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:06.210
Speaker 1: If you look at critiques of the
way that Ulbricht was ultimately prosecuted,

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:12.990
it seems what most often comes up is something
along the lines of, you know, the drug part.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:17.460
Increasingly, there is a view that drugs should
be decriminalized.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:22.820
At minimum, many should actually be legalized
more commonly with cannabis than with cocaine.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:27.710
But this is sort of like the way things have
kind of moved over the last decade, 15 years,

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:32.710
particularly libertarians like Ulbricht subscribe
to that ideology.

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:37.670
And there are many who say that were it only
the drugs alone, really what was happening

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:39.960
shouldn't actually have been against the law.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.340
However it went over into.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:46.449
You could hire hitmen on the Silk Road and
it went over into all this other stuff that

00:06:46.449 --> 00:06:50.010
I think is more universally thought of as
like, you know, that that stuff definitely

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:54.870
shouldn't be legal when someone like you or
others in the FBI are investigating something

00:06:54.870 --> 00:07:01.490
like this, how much or does it matter at all
what your personal feelings are about?

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:06.330
For example, which drugs, if any, should be
illegal or decriminalized or legalized, or

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:10.470
does it quite literally never enter into the
work as you are investigating something like

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:11.470
this?

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:13.120
Speaker 2: It really never enters into the
work.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.910
I mean, so I was assigned to New York.

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:17.560
I was especially in New York.

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:20.460
And and I like to say in New York, you know,
it's so specialized.

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:23.830
There's, you know, let's I jokingly say there's
a bank robbery.

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:26.780
And the first question is, was the bank robber
left handed or right handed?

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:29.880
Because there's one squad handles left in
bank robbery, the other side handles right

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:30.880
handed.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:31.880
Right.

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:32.880
You know, you're put on a squad.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:36.379
And we were very we were criminal cyber intrusions.

00:07:36.379 --> 00:07:38.930
So we were the hacking criminal hacking crew.

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:39.930
That was that was our squad.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:42.440
And there's another squad that did national
security hacking.

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:48.409
So really, you know, I was just investigating
hacking and really Tore had come up We had

00:07:48.409 --> 00:07:53.341
partnered up with start with Southern District
of New York, who had a case with the DEA at

00:07:53.341 --> 00:07:55.120
the time and then brought in the IRS.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:08:01.600
And so really I was looking at 18 U.S.C. 1030
violation of hacking rules.

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:06.630
And that was one of the things sold on Silk
Road was was hacking tools or hacks for hire.

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:09.220
And so that was sort of the nexus.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:12.900
But really, when we opened up the Silk Road
case, we opened up 25 of the Onion sites and

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:18.430
it really was more looking into using Tor
as a way of hiding criminal activity.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:24.750
Speaker 1: When you see the sentence that
Ulbricht ultimately got, which was, I believe,

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:27.100
two life sentences without the possibility
of parole.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:28.759
Speaker 2: Does 40 years.

00:08:28.759 --> 00:08:30.599
Speaker 1: Plus 40 years.

00:08:30.599 --> 00:08:31.599
Okay.

00:08:31.599 --> 00:08:33.380
Does that seem like the right sentence for
what he did?

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:35.349
Speaker 2: That is not my decision.

00:08:35.349 --> 00:08:39.300
You know, I'm a part of the you know, thank
God the government has different branches.

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:43.659
You know, I'm part of the I was part of the
executive branch where no doubt the laws,

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:47.350
you know, the legislative branch does the
you know what laws there are.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:49.649
And the judicial branch does come up with
the punishments.

00:08:49.649 --> 00:08:50.940
So that that's where we are.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:51.940
My scope of expertise.

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:52.940
Speaker 1: No.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:54.959
But as a guy who now is out and just might
have an opinion.

00:08:54.959 --> 00:08:57.310
Speaker 2: I really don't have an opinion
on it.

00:08:57.310 --> 00:08:58.310
Yeah.

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:00.430
Speaker 1: And I'm not suggesting I don't
know that.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:02.990
I mean, sometimes people say that because
they don't want to answer.

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:07.790
I actually am open to the idea that you really
just that's not the way you think about these

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:08.790
things.

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:10.089
You have a particular role in this whole thing.

00:09:10.089 --> 00:09:11.350
Speaker 2: Yeah, certainly.

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:17.390
I mean, I'm not I wouldn't be upset if someone
I arrested, you know, was let go.

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:22.279
I mean, I. I arrested Hector months ago or
he spent months in jail, was facing hundred

00:09:22.279 --> 00:09:23.649
25 years, but did a great job.

00:09:23.649 --> 00:09:25.380
I'm not offended by all the work I put in.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:26.829
And he got out and didn't got got.

00:09:26.829 --> 00:09:30.649
He was only in prison for for nine months
for for what he did.

00:09:30.649 --> 00:09:33.010
You know, that doesn't offend me in any sort
of way.

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:35.470
So, yeah, I really don't have an opinion on
it.

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:38.970
Speaker 1: What's the current state of Darkweb
marketplaces?

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:42.779
Because it's not really a topic in the news
right now, but it's sort of like by definition,

00:09:42.779 --> 00:09:46.380
the idea is it's dark web, so you don't necessarily
know about it.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:47.690
These aren't indexed websites.

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:49.700
You need a special browser to access it.

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:52.030
Do you know much about the current state of
these types of marketplaces?

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:53.150
Speaker 2: I do.

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:54.490
I'm still involved in them.

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:59.260
So, yeah, you know, you would ask me right
after Silk Road and right after Ross got sentenced

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:02.269
to all that, you know, two life sentences
in 40 years, I would have said it would have

00:10:02.269 --> 00:10:03.269
gone down.

00:10:03.269 --> 00:10:04.310
I would no one would do.

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:05.310
This is crazy.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:07.550
But it actually exploded right after that.

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:11.970
There was a big state of, you know, people
setting up these sites and stealing people's

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:13.370
cryptocurrency.

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:16.850
But now it's become into, you know, there's
places, you know, and I don't want to tell

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:18.180
kids how to get there.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:19.180
Right.

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:22.841
You know, But but there are many, many dark
money Web websites out there that you can

00:10:22.841 --> 00:10:26.050
still still do many more than there were back
in the day.

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:30.300
Speaker 1: Are they ever based in the United
States or is that just like the height of

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:32.070
ignorance to approach it that way?

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:36.180
Speaker 2: I think some people probably still
do it in the United States.

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:38.180
You know, it's it's sort of a Whac-A-Mole.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:41.720
Someone makes a mistake, a configuration issue
or something like that, and law enforcement

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:42.990
gets them.

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:46.680
But the latest takedowns haven't really used
infrastructure in the United States that I've

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:47.680
seen.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:52.510
Speaker 1: You mentioned the connection to
crypto and that crypto was often the payment

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:54.700
method that was used.

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:59.769
One of the interesting and full disclosure,
I have no emotional attachment to this question.

00:10:59.769 --> 00:11:01.790
I also have no opinion.

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:07.740
I was not early, but you know, 2016 I bought
some bitcoin and it did really well and I've

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:11.490
mostly sold all of it and I have no opinion
as to where it's going or how much it's used

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:12.910
for criminality.

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:18.080
One of the arguments that was made early about
Bitcoin was it's overwhelmingly used for criminal

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:19.350
activities.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:26.550
But my understanding is that when the Silk
Road was shut down, the throughput of transactions

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:31.180
barely changed at all, which sort of would
be a counterpoint to the idea that Bitcoin

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:33.810
was primarily being used for criminal transactions.

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:39.800
Did you, in your investigative work, come
to have an opinion about that and how regularly

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:43.389
criminal motivations were at play for Bitcoin
transactions?

00:11:43.389 --> 00:11:48.380
Speaker 2: So, you know, in my world as an
FBI agent, I only looked at criminal activity.

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:50.390
So yeah, I saw it attached to everything.

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:52.680
I mean, it really was the birth of ransomware.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:57.450
You mean we've had encryption for, you know,
75 years, you know, digital encryption and

00:11:57.450 --> 00:12:02.200
that sort of thing, all the way back to, you
know, the Nazis encryption, you know, the

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:04.250
Enigma machines, you know, So that's not new.

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:08.000
What really was the rise for ransomware was
the popularity of cryptocurrency.

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:13.130
So, you know, we're fighting we're still fighting
to that to this day on that sort of thing.

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:16.079
So, you know, I am a technologist.

00:12:16.079 --> 00:12:17.390
I liked cryptocurrency.

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:18.510
I like I see the point of it.

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:22.730
I like that, you know, two people can meet
in a far off place and make a transaction

00:12:22.730 --> 00:12:27.000
and it's locked in place within, you know,
60 minutes at most.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:29.260
But I see a lot of criminal activity connected
to it.

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:36.010
Speaker 1: What are you seeing now in terms
of the trends of the cyber threats, scams,

00:12:36.010 --> 00:12:37.839
phishing at the individual level?

00:12:37.839 --> 00:12:42.180
If you're able to comment on it, you know,
about five years ago, I was getting a lot

00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:46.880
of the, hey, you know, tax money and we're
sending the police to your house, to which

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:48.089
I would say send them.

00:12:48.089 --> 00:12:49.089
Sounds interesting.

00:12:49.089 --> 00:12:50.089
Obviously.

00:12:50.089 --> 00:12:51.560
I know I don't you know, I'm not giving you
any money.

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:53.870
Social Security scams.

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:58.459
Give us access to your computer and we'll
like log into your bank, that sort of stuff.

00:12:58.459 --> 00:13:00.630
I'm not being targeted by that anymore.

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:05.460
I'm getting a lot more sort of like telemarketing
stuff, like wanting to sell me warranties

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:08.230
on vehicles I don't even have and this sort
of thing.

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:09.620
What are the trends right now?

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:10.620
What's going on?

